## Results

Ran the deployment model (Random Forest, fit on full March data) across all
99,197 eligible items (≥100 impressions). 58,480 items — 59.0% of the
eligible pool — were flagged for action (score > 0.05pp CTR gap).

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Week 4 baseline MAE | 0.00277 |
| Week 5 grouped-split MAE | 0.00268 (3.2% improvement) |
| Week 6 within-March MAE | 0.00243 |
| Week 6 time-aware (Feb→March) MAE | 0.00243 (0.0% gap) |
| Queue size | 58,480 / 99,197 (59.0%) |

**Permutation importance (deployment model, 75/25 random eval split):**

| Feature | Importance |
|---|---|
| avg_position | 0.1336 |
| impressions | 0.0302 |
| search_volume | 0.0288 |
| category_count | 0.0243 |
| main_intent | 0.0047 |

`avg_position` remains the dominant signal, consistent with every prior week.
Absolute magnitudes are higher here than in Week 5 (0.0799) because this
importance was computed on the deployment model's own random eval split,
not the Week 5 grouped-by-client split — same signal ranking, different
eval setup, noted here so it doesn't read as a contradiction.

**Caveat:** every row visible in the top-15 (sorted by score descending)
has `actual_ctr = 0.0` — expected, since sorting by score surfaces the
"high predicted, zero actual" cohort first. Not yet confirmed whether
nonzero-but-low-CTR items are meaningfully represented further down the
queue; worth a quick `ranked_queue["actual_ctr"].describe()` check before
finalizing the Week 8 paper.
